      "text": "First, let's rewrite both equations in slope-intercept form \\( y = mx + b \\), where \\( m \\) represents the slope of the line.\n\nStarting with the given equations:\n1. \\( y = 2x + 13 \\)\n2. \\( 6y + ax = 6 \\)\n\nFor the first equation, \\( y = 2x + 13 \\), the slope \\( m \\) is already given:\n\\[ m_1 = 2 \\]\n\nNext, consider the second equation \\( 6y + ax = 6 \\). We need to solve for \\( y \\) in terms of \\( x \\).\n\nFirst, isolate the \\( y \\) variable:\n\\[\n6y + ax = 6\n\\]\nSubtract \\( ax \\) from both sides:\n\\[\n6y = -ax + 6\n\\]\nDivide both sides by 6:\n\\[\ny = -\\frac{a}{6}x + 1\n\\]\nThis gives us the slope for the second equation:\n\\[\nm_2 = -\\frac{a}{6}\n\\]\n\nFor the lines to be perpendicular, the product of their slopes must be \\(-1\\). That is:\n\\[\nm_1 \\cdot m_2 = -1\n\\]\n\nSubstitute the slopes:\n\\[\n2 \\cdot \\left( -\\frac{a}{6} \\right) = -1\n\\]\n\nSimplify the left-hand side:\n\\[\n-\\frac{2a}{6} = -1\n\\]\n\\[\n-\\frac{a}{3} = -1\n\\]\n\nTo solve for \\( a \\):\n\\[\n\\frac{a}{3} = 1\n\\]\nMultiply both sides by 3:\n\\[\na = 3\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\( a \\) that makes the lines perpendicular is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{3}\n\\]",
