      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the value of \\( a \\) such that the given lines are perpendicular, we first need to understand the conditions for two lines to be perpendicular. Two lines are perpendicular if the product of their slopes is \\(-1\\).\n\nThe given equations of the lines are:\n\\[ y = 2x + 13 \\]\n\\[ 6y + ax = 6 \\]\n\nFirst, we find the slope of the line \\( y = 2x + 13 \\). The equation is already in slope-intercept form \\( y = mx + b \\), where \\( m \\) is the slope. Therefore, the slope of this line is \\( 2 \\).\n\nNext, we convert the second equation \\( 6y + ax = 6 \\) into slope-intercept form \\( y = mx + b \\). We do this by isolating \\( y \\):\n\\[ 6y + ax = 6 \\]\n\\[ 6y = -ax + 6 \\]\n\\[ y = -\\frac{a}{6}x + 1 \\]\nSo, the slope of this line is \\( -\\frac{a}{6} \\).\n\nFor the lines to be perpendicular, the product of their slopes must be \\(-1\\):\n\\[ 2 \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{a}{6}\\right) = -1 \\]\n\\[ -\\frac{2a}{6} = -1 \\]\n\\[ -\\frac{a}{3} = -1 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{a}{3} = 1 \\]\n\\[ a = 3 \\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( a \\) that makes the lines perpendicular is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).",
